 "ask_club": {
  "q": "How have Brighton's style and results changed since the start of the season?",
  "secs": 32,
  "steps": [
   "Found Brighton & Hove Albion",
   "Read their nine league games, the first four against the last four",
   "Read how they play against the rest of the league"
  ],
  "head": {
   "kick": "Premier League · 2026/2027",
   "name": "Brighton & Hove Albion",
   "mgr": "Manager Fabian Hürzeler",
   "pos": "3rd",
   "pts": "19 points from 9 games",
   "form": [
    "w",
    "w",
    "w",
    "l",
    "w"
   ],
   "line": [
    "6 won, 1 drawn, 2 lost",
    "Goal difference +17",
    "5 points off the top"
   ],
   "cr": "img/t78.webp"
  },
  "paras": [
   "<p>Brighton under Fabian Hurzeler are in third place after nine games (6W, 1D, 2L, 24 goals scored, 7 conceded), and the most interesting story of their season is a deliberate, marked shift in how they are playing, and it has made them <i>more</i> effective, not less.</p>",
   "<h4>Early season: a classic possession side</h4>",
   "<p>In the first four games (Aston Villa through Coventry), Brighton averaged <b>70.8% possession</b>, 88.8% pass accuracy and 19.5 shots per game. They were playing the expansive, patient build-up football that has been the club's identity for years. The results were good, 1.75 points per game, but they were also leaking: 1.25 goals conceded per game in that stretch, including a 4-3 defeat at Chelsea in round two when they had 74% of the ball and still lost.</p>",
   "<h4>Recent games: a different animal</h4>",
   "<p>In the last four matches, possession has dropped to <b>49.2%</b> and pass accuracy to 78%. Tackles have jumped from 12 to 17 per game. They look more direct, more combative and far harder to hurt. Goals conceded: <b>0.5 per game</b>. Points per game: <b>2.25</b>. Six clean sheets in nine games overall, with the clean sheet percentage standing at 66.7%.</p>",
   "<p>The most striking individual game in that run was the 1-0 win at Manchester City (round 9), where Brighton had just 38% of the ball and seven shots, yet won. The 5-0 home rout of Crystal Palace (round 7) sits alongside that, where they commanded 57% possession, so it is not a rigid system, more a willingness to adapt to the opponent.</p>"
  ]
 },
